#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12637b1d-ca03-568f-b1fc-6a626eec88de" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Given the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2737e6bc-5372-50a0-bf2f-59a91f571a57" :question/math-academy-id "e-4520" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n${-2x + 5y - 2z =-2; - 3y + z =-2; 3x - 7y + 3z = 3$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$. Given that $\\mathbf x = \\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z$?\n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAx &= b \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= A^{-1}b \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=3, y=0, z=-2\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx+y+z=3+0+(-2)=1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091973 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad1721d-26a4-5459-bc81-297ff0e4d678" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations By Calculating the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "d32f2604-fc3f-5e80-8085-483e6fd9a3ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4521" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations using matrices:\n${3y + x + z = 0; z + 4y - 5 = 2; 1 + 2x - y =-3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are in the correct order and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n{x + 3y + z = 0; 0x + 4y + z = 7; 2x - y + 0z =-4\n$$\n\nNext, we write the system in the form\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1*: Find the determinant of $A$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} &= 1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}- 3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+ 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (1) - 3(-2) + (-8) \\\\\n&= 1 + 6 - 8 \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2*: Compute the matrix of minors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= [\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid ] \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3*: Find the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Step 4*: Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Step 5*: Apply the inverse matrix formula.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate the solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= A^{-1}b \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3$, $y=10$, $z=-33$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091974 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d0cda54-4a4b-5255-b375-ce5e7b604dff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Matrix Equation Using Algebraic Manipulation"}] :question/id #uuid "06a50eb4-4976-55fd-aa44-6509b27eccb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4604" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $B\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d}$, where $A$ and $B$ are $10 \\times 10$ non-singular matrices, while $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are $10 \\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $\\mathbf{x}$." :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse $B^{-1}$. Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation\n\n$$\nB\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d}\n$$\n\nby $B^{-1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBx &= A^{-1}d \\\\\nB^{-1}Bx &= B^{-1}A^{-1}d \\\\\nI_{10}x &= B^{-1}A^{-1}d \\\\\nx &= B^{-1}A^{-1}d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad1721d-26a4-5459-bc81-297ff0e4d678" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations By Calculating the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8049c2ee-25bd-5c6e-8a35-d29ae149220b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090402} {:answer/id #uuid "9703bb69-958a-5f71-b6a7-b9ce5ff0d2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090403} {:answer/id #uuid "04a5b1b3-c9da-5987-bed2-1ca5b006038c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090404} {:answer/id #uuid "29a0ecf4-1d6e-54cb-812a-79bf8141156e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090405} {:answer/id #uuid "4b66942a-3147-5d51-af6c-504f0820a25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a5b1b3-c9da-5987-bed2-1ca5b006038c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090404} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed83dae-ebe6-54f7-ba2a-6c67595b0eff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090401}] :question/id #uuid "be9d95c2-a710-5571-987b-8cd6ddd0d0ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-42449" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n${2x - 3 + z = 0; 5y - 1 + 3x = 2z + 1; 3z + 2x - 1 = 2y$\nFind $x + y + z$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d0cda54-4a4b-5255-b375-ce5e7b604dff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Matrix Equation Using Algebraic Manipulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7d6b37b-20a4-5dae-9714-7ccadee39dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $D^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091020} {:answer/id #uuid "da87e365-82b4-5b06-b200-fbccc58ed546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $D$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091021} {:answer/id #uuid "db19c443-a1fc-5b8b-bebd-6028e50cf51a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $I_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091022} {:answer/id #uuid "594c9f9a-8850-560a-9bf6-5ccbca1af66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D^{-1}$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091023} {:answer/id #uuid "773b5340-5235-53ae-8b22-5005d3594900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$I_{3}$ and $D^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594c9f9a-8850-560a-9bf6-5ccbca1af66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D^{-1}$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091023} :answer-field/id #uuid "405e54ce-2163-5b71-b891-698847c6dda9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091019}] :question/id #uuid "15bdd24c-e192-58dc-b8c4-b4c9d91ba43f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78442" :question/problem "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $Dy = w$. Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $y$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $w$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$Dy|= w; D^{-1}Dy|= D - 1w; I_{3}y|= D^{-1}w; D|= D^{-1}w$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12637b1d-ca03-568f-b1fc-6a626eec88de" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Given the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87f943ff-0c2a-5b06-8bef-42ab98671fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091050} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f36725-cb69-5eb2-8330-0b8f600b011d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091051} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c1a2fe-fb8d-5338-a8d0-e73336e6ce9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091052} {:answer/id #uuid "00a602e6-98bf-544e-a82e-58cca7fcff8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091053} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad8560c-81b8-5288-8ccd-f8a29d4510cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1f36725-cb69-5eb2-8330-0b8f600b011d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091051} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13343dd-0fe9-59b1-847f-d9dc33737c2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091049}] :question/id #uuid "35f6dc89-f4a6-5953-a50e-5f17ce4b6254" :question/math-academy-id "q-78557" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n${x - y + 3z = 2; x - 2y - 5z =-1; 2x - 3y + 2z = 1$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax = b$. Given that $x = ⟨x, y, z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x + y + z$?\n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n$A^{-1} =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-19 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad1721d-26a4-5459-bc81-297ff0e4d678" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations By Calculating the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deeb6edb-82fd-5276-aab1-32b7cc91913d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-42, y =-35, z = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091770} {:answer/id #uuid "fac7a9c3-7dea-502a-80e9-9104b2f08159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 42, y = 35, z = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091771} {:answer/id #uuid "26c8e715-eef7-5ca7-ba2d-10026ad1eb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 22, y =-45, z =-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091772} {:answer/id #uuid "3de52c21-e4ea-5e84-bb5e-a9bb1dc4fcca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 42, y =-35, z = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091773} {:answer/id #uuid "476bebb7-c79c-5a3a-b63d-e813bd835ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 42, y =-35, z =-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3de52c21-e4ea-5e84-bb5e-a9bb1dc4fcca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 42, y =-35, z = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091773} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c23960-37e8-5145-a308-b73deba2d751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091769}] :question/id #uuid "3e2cd33f-1df6-521c-a13d-e818a65472f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-9722" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n${2y + 3z + x =-1; 4z + y = 1; 5x + 6y - 3 =-3$"}]}
